---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { profilesForDomain } from '../../data/profiles';

const itProfiles = profilesForDomain('it');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="IT Governance Platform"
  description="An IT governance platform uniting enterprise architecture, continuous compliance automation, and impact analysis in one governed source of truth your teams and AI agents can act on."
  pageType="Domain"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Poesis for IT · Domain</p>
      <h1>The IT governance platform where definitions do the governing.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Enterprise architecture repositories describe your systems. Compliance tooling checklists them. Poesis <em>defines</em> them — one governed, machine-readable source of truth for your entire IT landscape, from which artifacts are generated, impacts simulated, and compliance continuously proven.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/solutions/itip"><Icon name="compass" /> Explore ITIP</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://docs.poesis.cloud/itip/"><Icon name="docs" /> Read the docs</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/contact"><Icon name="message" /> Talk to us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--ink">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The mental model</p>
      <h2>What Kubernetes did for infrastructure, Poesis does for IT governance.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Kubernetes let you <em>declare</em> the desired state of your infrastructure and trust a reconciliation loop to converge reality toward it. Poesis extends that principle to everything that governs your IT: architectures, obligations, constraints, and processes become declared definitions — continuously evaluated against the real system, with drift observed the moment it appears and humans disposing of every consequential change.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What it replaces</p>
        <h2>Five practices, one governed model.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Each of these is sold today as a separate tool with a separate copy of the truth. In Poesis they are facets of the same governed definitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">Enterprise architecture</span>
          <h3>An EA repository that cannot drift.</h3>
          <p>Instead of an architecture wiki maintained by hand, your landscape is a governed inventory of typed definitions — sourced from the code, contracts, and infrastructure that actually run, so the map never detaches from the territory.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">Compliance automation</span>
          <h3>Compliance observed, not reconstructed.</h3>
          <p>Continuous appraisal indicators compute measures and findings from the model across governance and meta-governance zones. Your regulatory posture — GDPR, NIS2, DORA, ISO — is a live dashboard, not a spreadsheet rebuilt before each audit.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">Impact analysis</span>
          <h3>See the blast radius before you commit.</h3>
          <p>Changes ripple through typed relations, so the impact of a decision is simulated from the model — traced through the definitions it touches, not guessed in a meeting.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">Artifact generation</span>
          <h3>Deliverables fall out of the model.</h3>
          <p>ADR packs, compliance evidence, architecture baselines, roadmaps — generated from the definitions, each artifact traceable back to the exact definitions it derives from.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">Truth sourcing</span>
          <h3>The model reflects what actually runs.</h3>
          <p>A bench of knowledge sources continuously reads your repositories, API contracts, SBOMs, and infrastructure and posts them as evidence-backed, confidence-scored contributions — so governance never drifts from implementation.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--feature card--static">
          <span class="tag">AI-ready governance</span>
          <h3>A source of truth AI agents can act on.</h3>
          <p>Every definition is typed by a GSM Archetype, so its meaning is explicit and machine-readable. Humans and AI agents reason and generate from the same governed account of what your systems are and must do.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it compares</p>
        <h2>Tool classes describe. Poesis defines.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Each tool class holds a partial, descriptive copy of the truth. Poesis holds the governed definition they all derive from.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-wrap">
        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Concern</th>
              <th scope="col">EA repository</th>
              <th scope="col">GRC suite</th>
              <th scope="col">CMDB / ITSM</th>
              <th scope="col" class="compare-poesis">Poesis for IT</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Enterprise architecture</th>
              <td>Hand-maintained inventory that drifts</td>
              <td>Out of scope</td>
              <td>Flat CI lists without architecture semantics</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">Governed inventory of typed definitions, sourced from what runs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Compliance automation</th>
              <td>Referenced documents</td>
              <td>Checklist snapshots rebuilt per audit</td>
              <td>GRC modules on declared controls</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">Posture computed continuously from the model</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Impact analysis</th>
              <td>Static diagrams</td>
              <td>Out of scope</td>
              <td>Dependency lists, CAB approvals</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">Blast radius simulated through typed relations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Artifact generation</th>
              <td>Manual exports and diagrams</td>
              <td>Audit reports</td>
              <td>CI reports</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">ADR packs, evidence, baselines, roadmaps — generated, traceable to definitions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Truth sourcing</th>
              <td>Surveys, imports, curation</td>
              <td>Integration evidence sampling</td>
              <td>Discovery scans, manual registration</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">Continuous sourcing from code, contracts, SBOMs, infra — confidence + provenance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">AI-ready governance</th>
              <td>Exports</td>
              <td>Reports</td>
              <td>CIs without semantics</td>
              <td class="compare-poesis">Typed, governed definitions humans and agents act on</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.25rem;text-align:center">Evaluating a specific tool? <a href="/it/alternatives/leanix/">LeanIX</a> · <a href="/it/alternatives/vanta/">Vanta</a> · <a href="/it/alternatives/servicenow/">ServiceNow</a> · <a href="/it/alternatives/backstage/">Backstage</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Who it's for</p>
        <h2>Every IT profile, one governed model.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Architects author, developers consult, ops watch drift, security proves posture — all on the same governed definitions, with no translation silos between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        {itProfiles.map((profile) => (
          <article class="card card--feature card--static">
            <span class="tag">{profile.name}</span>
            <h3>{profile.hook}</h3>
            <p>{profile.body}</p>
            <p><a href={profile.cta.href} data-profile-cta={profile.id}>{profile.cta.label} →</a></p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Built on a vendor-neutral standard</p>
        <h2>Governance you can inspect, not a black box.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Poesis for IT is delivered by <a href="/solutions/itip">ITIP</a>, the first domain application built on the <a href="/solutions/sie">SIE</a> engine and the <a href="/solutions/gsm">GSM</a> standard — the vendor-neutral model for defining and governing systems, the way OpenTelemetry standardized observing them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cta" style="margin-top:1.5rem;justify-content:center">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/solutions/itip"><Icon name="compass" /> Explore ITIP</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/insights/continuous-regulatory-compliance"><Icon name="book-open" /> Read: continuous regulatory compliance</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/insights/definition-versus-description"><Icon name="book-open" /> Read: definition versus description</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
